    <div class="panel panel-default">
        <div class="panel-heading">News Stream</div>
            
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>Initial Claims Above Forecasts in the Latest Week</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filling for unemployment benefits was unchanged at 884K in the week ended September 5th, the same as an upwardly revised 884K in the previous week and above market forecasts of 846 thousand. It is the first time since March that claims stay below 1 million for two straight weeks although the number still remains elevated. The 4-week moving average was 970,750, a decrease of 21,750 from the previous week. Based on non-seasonally adjusted data, biggest increases in claims were seen in California (17.95K), Texas (9.65K) and Puerto Rico (9.4K) while main decreases were seen in Florida (-9K), Kentucky (-7.5K) and Michigan (-5.6K). Meanwhile, continuing claims went up to 13.38 million from 13.29 million, above forecasts of 12.925 million. </div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2020-09-10</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>Initial Claims Seen Below 1 Million for 2nd Week</b>
                    <div class="comment more">846 thousand American workers are expected to have filed unemployment benefits during the week ending September 5th, compared to 881 thousand in the previous week. It would be the first time since March that claims come below 1 million for two straight weeks although the number still remains elevated. Since mid-March when the coronavirus crisis started, more than 58 million Americans have asked for jobless benefits. Meanwhile, continuing claims are projected to fall to 12.9 million on August 29th from 13.3 million.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2020-09-10</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>Initial Claims Fall on Changes in Seasonal Adjustments</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filling for unemployment benefits fell to 881K in the week ended August 29th, after the Labor Department changed its seasonal adjustment methodology to smooth the swings in employment. It is the lowest level since the effects of the pandemic started to being felt back in March and below market expectations of 950K. Still, it remained well above 665K filed at the peak of the Great Recession in March 2009, as the labor market continued to struggle amid rising COVID-19 cases. Initial claims started to fall in May, as many non-essential businesses started to reopen following weeks of closure due to the pandemic. Beginning this week, the Department of Labor changed the methodology used to seasonally adjust the claims to reflect additive factors as opposed to multiplicative factors. Additive seasonal adjustment factors are preferred in times of great uncertainty since they tend to more accurately track seasonal fluctuations in the series and have smaller revisions.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2020-09-03</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>Initial Claims Could Fall Well Below 1M due to Adjustments</b>
                    <div class="comment more">950 thousand American workers are expected to have filed unemployment benefits during the week ending August 29th, compared to 1.006 million in the previous week and remaining far above 665,000 filed at the peak of the Great Recession in March 2009. However, the drop in claims can be bigger as the Labor Department decided now to make changes in the seasonal adjustment method to reflect swings in the numbers. Since mid-March when the coronavirus crisis started, more than 58 million Americans have asked for jobless benefits. Meanwhile, continuing claims are projected to fall to 14 million on August 22nd from 14.5 million.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2020-09-03</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                 
            
